{"crc":"3390813e","kind":"Candidate","payload":{"response":"Looking at the issue, the problem is that when a URL pattern has optional groups (like `(?P<format>(html|json|xml))?`), the regex match can return `None` for that group. The current code in `RegexPattern.match()` filters out `None` values from kwargs, but when all named groups are `None`, it falls back to using positional args from `match.groups()`, which includes the `None` values. This causes the view to receive extra positional arguments.\n\nThe fix should be in `RegexPattern.match()` to filter out `None` values from both kwargs and args.\n\n# path: django/urls/resolvers.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def match(self, path):\n        match = self.regex.search(path)\n        if match:\n            # If there are any named groups, use those as kwargs, ignoring\n            # non-named groups. Otherwise, pass all non-named arguments as\n            # positional arguments.\n            kwargs = {k: v for k, v in match.groupdict().items() if v is not None}\n            args = () if kwargs else match.groups()\n            return path[match.end():], args, kwargs\n        return None\n=======\n    def match(self, path):\n        match = self.regex.search(path)\n        if match:\n            # If there are any named groups, use those as kwargs, ignoring\n            # non-named groups. Otherwise, pass all non-named arguments as\n            # positional arguments.\n            kwargs = match.groupdict()\n            args = ()\n            if kwargs:\n                kwargs = {k: v for k, v in kwargs.items() if v is not None}\n            else:\n                args = tuple(v for v in match.groups() if v is not None)\n            return path[match.end():], args, kwargs\n        return None\n>>>>>>> REPLACE","round":1,"slot":0},"producer":{"instance":"01KZQWAPM26CFPGRM8SANWRNHG","kind":"drafter","parent":"01KZQWAPKXFZNH21TCR3W4RD6F"},"schema":"Candidate@1","seq":14,"t":1786434120.55218}
